Title: "Collection Expressions"
Id: "110b77c2-d148-4adf-a70a-92ddad3cda11"

Text: "```mlg-fragment
SetExpression ::= \"{\" CollectionTarget \":\" \"...\" \"}\"
                | \"{\" CollectionTarget \":\" Expression ((\",\" | \";\") Expression)* (\"|\" SetPredicate)? \"}\"
CollectionTarget ::= SetTarget | Expression
SetTarget ::= name | placeholder-form | tuple-target | function-target | SetTarget \"::=\" SetTarget
SetPredicate ::= Expression | SetTarget \":=\" Expression
```

The binder before `:` may be a name, tuple, function form, or introduction
form:

```mlg-fragment
{x_ : x_ is \\real}
{x_ : x_ is \\real | x_ = y}
{(p_, q_) : ...}
{z_ ::= (a_, b_) : a_ \"in\" A; b_ \"in\" B | z_ := \\ordered.pair:of{a_}:and{b_}}
```

Any **expression** may also be the produced member to the left of the colon.
Placeholder names within that expression are local to the collection, while
ordinary names continue to refer to the surrounding scope:

```mlg-fragment
{\\equivalence.class:of{x_}:over{R} : x_ \"in\" X}
```

Specifications before `|` may be separated by commas or semicolons.
Definitions using `:=` belong in the predicate branch after `|`, not in the
specification list before it.

The bare ellipsis form `{x_ : ...}` places no constraint on the elements. It is
useful in a `from:` declaration that describes the shape of an accepted build
source without adding element constraints.

A collection literal also satisfies a command's nominal type requirement
structurally: the checker compares it against the required type's described
element pattern and element specs."
Id: "0bc47296-26c5-4616-b6d9-742bd034d436"
